WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.290
‘No bug left behind’ is the motto of this ant species,

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.960
found in southern regions of Africa.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.980
When the ants hunt termites—a risky prey—

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.080
the colony sticks together,

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.880
marching into battle in columns,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.490
and making sure even injured ants make it back.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.560
Large ants called “majors”

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.820
break up soil around termite nests during a raid,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.780
while smaller “minor” ants slip through the cracks

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.980
to extract their meal.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.780
Soldier termites fight back,

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:37.240
and ants lose limbs and antennae in the scuffle.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.780
After the fight, the ants line up to go home,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.000
carrying termite carcasses along for a post-battle feast.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.400
When predators, like spiders,

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.420
see injured ants struggling home,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.720
they know they’re in for an easy snack.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.240
So majors stick around after the fight,

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.080
tracking the scent of a pheromone that signals injury.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.060
When an injured ant is found,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.860
the major grabs the fallen soldier in its mandibles

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.940
and carries it home to recover safely in the nest.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.340
To see what became of those injured ants,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.160
researches created laboratory nests

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.660
and hunting grounds

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.080
and marked the injured with paint.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.600
Ninety-five percent of those hurt ants

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.420
marched alongside their brethren on future raids.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.520
The number of ants rescued each day

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.200
is about the same as daily ant births,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.240
meaning rescues help the colony stay strong

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.800
and prepared to fight another day.

